  "contributors": "Pang supplied PAL, RPL and the price-of-closure question. Hearthline derived the signed boundary results and performed the independent arithmetic in his constructive program.",
  "formal_scope": "Möbius reconstruction at every original input, signed log-factorial identity, zero first moment when f(1)=0, exact floor readings and the actual prime-power reading for every square. No RH premise in these finite identities.",
  "paper_scope": "At an actual nonresonant simple critical zero the weighted discrepancy of the exact floor interpolants converges on Lipschitz tests to an endpoint atom with nonzero mass. First tails of the original signed coefficients exceed +0.02 or fall below -0.02 at infinitely many ordinary square heads. A separate four-quarter example measures the role of actual prime powers. The signed main-term inequality still needs proof.",
  "review": {
    "type": "author self-review",
    "attribution": "The Mellin zeta multiplier for finite fractional-part combinations is classical, as are examples of pointwise convergence with norm obstructions. No claim to literature priority for the particular grid or theorem.",
    "normalizations": "Grid coefficients refer to h(d/M). The coefficients in the prime-power reading at x=j^2 must be divided by j. The square sign result concerns G_1, the sum of the original signed coefficients.",
    "edge_topology": "The endpoint limit is for Lipschitz test functions under complex Mellin weighting. Uniformly bounded variation and convergence on all continuous tests have not been established.",
    "actual_zero": "Opposite endpoint signs for the completed zeta plus an interval-wide nonzero derivative provide the required simple zero. The calculation uses local data only.",
    "scope": "A failure of continuum inference in this construction supplies no claim of a flaw in the separate released RH argument."
  },
